            "square with the same area as a given circle, using only an "
            "unmarked straightedge and compass. If the circle radius is r, "
            "the required square side s satisfies s² = πr², so "
            "s = r√π. Exact construction is impossible with those tools "
            "because π is transcendental, whereas constructible lengths "
            "are algebraic over the rationals. Numerical approximations "
            "are possible."
        )

    # Metric units of area.
    if "metric units of area" in low or "metric area units" in low:
        return (
            "METRIC UNITS OF AREA — Area conversion factors are squared "
            "because both dimensions are converted.\n"
            "- 1 m² = 100 dm² = 10,000 cm² = 1,000,000 mm².\n"
            "- 1 hectare = 10,000 m².\n"
            "- 1 km² = 1,000,000 m².\n"
            "To convert from m² to cm², multiply by 100² = 10,000; do not "
            "use the linear conversion factor 100 only."
        )

    # Golden rectangle.
    if "golden rectangle" in low:
        phi = (1 + math.sqrt(5)) / 2
        return (
            "THE GOLDEN RECTANGLE — A golden rectangle has long side a and "
            "short side b with a/b = φ, where φ = (1+√5)/2 ≈ "
            f"{fmt(phi)}. If the short side is b, the long side is φb. "
            "Removing a square of side b leaves a smaller golden rectangle "
            "because (a-b)/b = 1/φ. This follows from φ² = φ + 1."
        )

    # Golden spiral.
    if "golden spiral" in low:
        return (
            "THE GOLDEN SPIRAL — An ideal logarithmic golden spiral expands "
            "by a factor of φ for each quarter-turn of 90°: "
            "r(θ) = a·φ^(2θ/π) when θ is in radians and a is a positive "
            "scale constant. Equivalently, r(θ) = a·exp((2 ln φ/π)θ). "
            "A common drawing approximation uses quarter-circles inside "
            "successively sized squares of a Fibonacci tiling; that is an "
            "approximation, not an exact logarithmic spiral."
        )

    # Approximating areas.
    if "approximating areas" in low or "approximate area" in low:
        return (
            "APPROXIMATING AREAS — For an irregular planar region, divide "
            "it into manageable shapes or use a grid.\n"
            "- Rectangle approximation: A ≈ sum of included cell areas.\n"
            "- Trapezoidal rule: A ≈ (Δx/2)[y₀ + 2(y₁+...+yₙ₋₁) + yₙ].\n"
            "- Simpson's rule for an even number n of subintervals: "
            "A ≈ (Δx/3)[y₀ + yₙ + 4(sum of odd-indexed y values) + "
            "2(sum of even-indexed interior y values)].\n"
            "More, narrower subintervals generally improve approximations "
            "for sufficiently smooth boundaries. State the method and "
            "sampling assumptions."
        )

    # Curve stitching.
    if "curve stitching" in low or "string art" in low:
        return (
            "CURVE STITCHING — Curve stitching creates a visual curve by "
